te region. Although controlled-rolled steel generally offers greater strength and a lower transition temperature than conventionally hot-rolled steel, its texture development can cause anisotropy, including plane anisotropy, and may lead to through-thickness embrittlement and separations. When the microstructure contains mixed fine and coarse alpha grains and/or upper bainite, low-temperature toughness can decrease. Ductility and toughness are also co-targeted properties in this context. [Data: Entities (28, 1188, 948); Relationships (2565, 2656, 29, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Silicon supports the strength–ductility balance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Si denotes silicon, a strong ferrite former in steel. In controlled-rolled steel, silicon exhibits a good strength–ductility relation at 0.3–0.5%, given in bracketed composition form as [SI: 0.003–0.005]. Si also exhibits a good strength–ductility relation in normalized type steel. Composition conditions such as [SI: 0] and [SI: 0.3] indicate silicon content of 0 and 0.3, respectively. The strength–ductility relation itself involves ductility, making Si relevant to the balance between strength and deformability in these steels. [Data: Entities (636, 942, 941, 943, 650, +more); Relationships (1947, 1951, 1949, 1262, 1263, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Thermal processing controls recrystallization and grain growth\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Reheating temperature is reduced to minimize recrystallization and grain growth during the delay period. The temperature at the commencement of the delay period is reduced by reducing reheating temperature. This thermal control complements Ti additions that slow grain growth and, in large quantities around ~0.1%, retard recrystallization of gamma. Together, these process levers help preserve the refined microstructure and property advantages of controlled-rolled steel, though they must be balanced against toughness and weldability limitations. [Data: Entities (1014, 1015, 952, 886); Relationships (2130, 2132, 1919, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Welding and heat-affected-zone toughness are key limitations\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A weldment contains a heat-affected-zone, a region of a weldment affected by heat. Ti decreases toughness of the heat-affected-zone and of the base steel. This means that Ti-bearing controlled-rolled steels can face toughness penalties in welded joints, even though Ti is indispensable for grain refinement and precipitation hardening in the base material. The welding-related limitation is therefore one of the most important practical tradeoffs in this community. [Data: Entities (925, 924, 923, 886); Relationships (1963, 1909, 1908, 1815)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Figure 34 anchors microalloy effects on strength\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Figure 34 shows the effects of Nb, V, and Ti on strength. It also specifically shows the effects of Nb on strength. This figure supports the broader finding that microalloying elements are not interchangeable; their type and quantity influence strength outcomes alongside processing and microstructure. In this community, Fig. 34 provides a data reference point linking microalloy additions to measured strength behavior. [Data: Entities (957); Relationships (2009)]\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 4653, "completion_tokens": 6223, "total_tokens": 10876, "compute_duration_seconds": 33.223246335983276, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}